<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace MeRezaRezaei\Teleframe\Schema;

use MeRezaRezaei\Teleframe\Core\MTProto\TL\TLRegistry;

/**
 * Which catalogued methods this client can actually put on the wire, and —
 * for the ones it cannot — WHY, in words a host can act on.
 *
 * THE DEFECT THIS EXISTS TO CLOSE
 * ===============================
 * `methods-mtproto.json` is a faithful record of what Telegram's schema
 * declares. It is generated by `bin/generate-method-schema.php` from the
 * committed `sources/api.tl` + `sources/mtproto.tl`, and it lists every
 * function those files declare after `---functions---`. That is correct as a
 * *catalogue*.
 *
 * It is NOT the same question as "can I call this?". A call has to be TL-encoded
 * through `TLRegistry`, and `TLRegistry` seeds itself from a DIFFERENT pair of
 * committed files: `sources/api_full.tl` + `sources/mtproto_full.tl`. The two
 * pairs are not the same layer — `api.tl` declares `// LAYER 229` and
 * `api_full.tl` declares `// LAYER 227` — and the gap is deliberate repo-wide
 * (the wire advertises 227; the catalogue documents 229; never "reconcile"
 * either number). A consequence nobody stated until now: the catalogue is
 * strictly BROADER than the wire, so a slice of it is advertised and then
 * throws deep inside an encoder.
 *
 * There is also a second, smaller gap that has nothing to do with layers: a
 * .tl function is not automatically a callable ENDPOINT. `invokeWithTakeout`
 * and its siblings declare a generic `{X:Type} ... query:!X = X` — they wrap
 * some other request. `req_pq` and `destroy_auth_key` come from `mtproto.tl`,
 * which is the service layer the connection speaks during the auth handshake,
 * not the schema layer an RPC address resolves to. None of these are things a
 * host dials by name; the client applies them for you.
 *
 * TWO QUESTIONS, NOT ONE
 * ======================
 * "Can a HOST encode and send this by name" and "is this meant to be DIALED by
 * name" are different questions, and one stamp cannot honestly answer both.
 * A `{X:Type}` wrapper is the case that proves it: the constructor is real and
 * the client hand-builds it (`TLRegistry::SCHEMA` carries `invokeWithLayer` and
 * `initConnection`), so it is genuinely on the wire — but no host can call it,
 * because binding `query:!X` requires the wrapped request, which is exactly the
 * thing the client supplies for you. So the two facts are separate here:
 *
 *   - `classify()` / `reason()` answer the DIAL-BY-NAME question. An envelope
 *     keeps its reason: it is real, and it is still not something a host dials.
 *   - `isEnvelope()` / `envelopeReason()` / `envelopes()` carry the shape.
 *   - `isEncodable()` asks the OTHER question, of the closure the encoder
 *     actually encodes through: does the registry hold a real signature for this
 *     name? The answer is yes for every mirror method AND for the two wrappers
 *     the client builds. `clientBuilt()` is that wrapper subset, and it is why
 *     an envelope can be on the wire and uncallable at the same time.
 *
 * A method is only ever reported unsupported when a host genuinely cannot send
 * it. `tests/Schema/CataloguedMethodsAreEncodableTest` pins both directions of
 * that against the real encoder.
 *
 * So: classify, do not guess. Every verdict below is read out of the committed
 * sources, never from memory of what these methods "should" do, and the
 * reasons name the file the evidence came from.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE RECONCILES THE LAYERS. This class reports the difference; it
 * never edits a layer number, a wire number, or a mirror.
 *
 * Parsing note: string functions only, no regex, mirroring the same line
 * rules `TLRegistry::mirrorFileLines()` applies to the mirror. That is
 * deliberate — the wire-mirror lookup must agree with the loader it describes,
 * and the test suite asserts the two agree on all 823 methods rather than
 * trusting a second implementation to drift quietly.
 */
final class WireSchemaCoverage
{
    /** A catalogued function the wire mirror can encode. */
    public const CALLABLE = 'callable';

    /**
     * A generic request WRAPPER (`{X:Type} ... query:!X = X`): not an endpoint.
     *
     * A shape, not a verdict about the wire: the client hand-builds the two
     * wrappers it needs, so an envelope may well be ENCODABLE (see
     * `isEncodable()`). Being one says "not dialed by name", not "broken".
     */
    public const ENVELOPE = 'envelope';

    /** An `mtproto.tl` service request: the connection layer owns it, not the RPC path. */
    public const SERVICE = 'service';

    /** Declared by the catalogue's layer, absent from the wire mirror's layer. */
    public const NOT_IN_WIRE_MIRROR = 'not-in-wire-mirror';

    /**
     * The sources `bin/generate-method-schema.php` builds the catalogue from,
     * in its own order. `api.tl` is the schema layer; `mtproto.tl` is the
     * service layer. Reading the catalogue's provenance from the same list the
     * generator uses is what makes SERVICE a fact rather than a guess.
     *
     * @var array<string, string> file => role ('schema'|'service')
     */
    private const CATALOGUE_SOURCES = [
        'api.tl' => 'schema',
        'mtproto.tl' => 'service',
    ];

    /**
     * The files `TLRegistry` actually seeds its encodable closure from.
     * These — NOT the catalogue sources — decide whether a method can be
     * serialized onto the wire.
     *
     * @var list<string>
     */
    private const WIRE_MIRROR_FILES = ['api_full.tl', 'mtproto_full.tl'];

    /** @var array<string, array{file: string, role: string, line: string}>|null */
    private static ?array $catalogueIndex = null;

    /** @var array<string, true>|null */
    private static ?array $wireMirrorIndex = null;

    /** @var array<string, int> */
    private static array $layerCache = [];

    /**
     * Classification buckets, with a human-readable reason for each
     * non-callable verdict. The ENVELOPE entry is NOT a "cannot call" reason:
     * it is served by `envelopeReason()`, and `reason()` withholds it from any
     * method that genuinely encodes.
     *
     * @return array<string, callable(string): string>
     */
    private static function reasons(): array
    {
        return [
            self::ENVELOPE => static fn (string $name): string => sprintf(
                '%s is a Telegram request ENVELOPE, not a callable endpoint: its .tl line in sources/%s declares a '
                .'generic {X:Type} and a bound query (!X = X), so it exists to wrap some other request. You never '
                .'dial it by name — the client wraps a request in it for you. Call the method you actually want '
                .'instead.',
                $name,
                self::catalogueIndex()[$name]['file'] ?? 'api.tl',
            ),
            self::SERVICE => static fn (string $name): string => sprintf(
                '%s is an MTProto SERVICE request declared in sources/%s, not a schema-layer RPC. The connection '
                .'layer speaks it directly (during the auth handshake, for example) and it is not addressable '
                .'through the method-call API, so there is nothing for a host to call here.',
                $name,
                self::catalogueIndex()[$name]['file'] ?? 'mtproto.tl',
            ),
            self::NOT_IN_WIRE_MIRROR => static fn (string $name): string => sprintf(
                '%s is declared by the schema catalogue (layer %d, sources/api.tl) but is absent from the wire mirror '
                .'this client encodes against (layer %d, sources/api_full.tl). Encoding goes through the mirror, so '
                .'this method can never reach Telegram. It is a layer-%d capability: Telegram servers speaking the '
                .'older layer do not implement it, and that gap is deliberate here — the wire advertises layer %d and '
                .'is not being moved.',
                $name,
                self::layerOf('api.tl'),
                self::layerOf('api_full.tl'),
                self::layerOf('api.tl'),
                self::layerOf('api_full.tl'),
            ),
        ];
    }

    /**
     * How the wire sees one catalogued method.
     *
     * Order matters. ENVELOPE is tested first because an envelope is present in
     * BOTH mirrors and would otherwise be misreported as a layer gap, which
     * would be a nicer-sounding lie than the truth.
     */
    public static function classify(string $name): string
    {
        if (isset(self::wireMirrorIndex()[$name])) {
            return self::CALLABLE;
        }

        $entry = self::catalogueIndex()[$name] ?? null;
        if ($entry === null) {
            // Not a catalogued function at all (a TYPE, or a name in neither
            // source). Nothing to claim either way — treat as callable so a
            // TYPE lookup is not reported as a method failure.
            return self::CALLABLE;
        }

        if (str_contains($entry['line'], '{X:Type}')) {
            return self::ENVELOPE;
        }

        if ($entry['role'] === 'service') {
            return self::SERVICE;
        }

        return self::NOT_IN_WIRE_MIRROR;
    }

    public static function isCallable(string $name): bool
    {
        return self::classify($name) === self::CALLABLE;
    }

    /**
     * Why a HOST cannot dial this method by name, or null when it can.
     *
     * This is the dial-by-name half of the contract, and it is not the same
     * question as `isEncodable()`. An envelope keeps its reason here: the
     * constructor is real and the client puts it on the wire, but binding
     * `query:!X` needs the wrapped request, so no host can send it. Demoting it
     * is therefore the truth, not a lie — but it is ALSO an envelope, and that
     * separate fact is available through `envelopeReason()`.
     */
    public static function reason(string $name): ?string
    {
        $verdict = self::classify($name);
        $reasons = self::reasons();

        if (! isset($reasons[$verdict])) {
            return null;
        }

        return $reasons[$verdict]($name);
    }

    /**
     * Is this a generic request WRAPPER rather than an endpoint?
     *
     * Read out of the catalogue's own .tl line, so it is a fact about the
     * schema, not an opinion about the client.
     */
    public static function isEnvelope(string $name): bool
    {
        $entry = self::catalogueIndex()[$name] ?? null;

        return $entry !== null && str_contains($entry['line'], '{X:Type}');
    }

    /**
     * Why a host should not dial this method by name — or null when it is not
     * an envelope. The same sentence `reason()` gives an envelope, exposed under
     * its own name so a caller can ask the SHAPE question without inferring it
     * from an unsupported verdict.
     */
    public static function envelopeReason(string $name): ?string
    {
        if (! self::isEnvelope($name)) {
            return null;
        }

        return self::reasons()[self::ENVELOPE]($name);
    }

    /**
     * Is this an envelope THE CLIENT BUILDS ITSELF?
     *
     * True for exactly the wrappers the client needs on every call and hands to
     * the encoder at a call site: `invokeWithLayer`, `initConnection`. They are
     * real constructors that really are on the wire, which is the whole reason
     * "is it on the wire" and "can a host dial it" are separate questions. Asked
     * of the registry, so the answer cannot drift from what the encoder holds.
     */
    public static function clientBuilt(string $name): bool
    {
        return self::isEnvelope($name) && TLRegistry::isRegistered($name);
    }

    /**
     * Does the closure the encoder encodes through hold a real signature for
     * this name?
     *
     * True for every method in the wire mirror, and for the two wrappers the
     * client hand-builds (the mirror index cannot see those: the loader treats
     * brace-bearing lines as declarations, not constructors). A name in neither
     * source — a TYPE, say — has nothing to prove.
     */
    public static function isEncodable(string $name): bool
    {
        if (isset(self::wireMirrorIndex()[$name])) {
            return true;
        }

        if (! isset(self::catalogueIndex()[$name])) {
            return true;
        }

        return TLRegistry::isRegistered($name);
    }

    /**
     * Every catalogued ENVELOPE, name => why it is not dialed by name.
     *
     * @param  list<string>  $names
     * @return array<string, string>
     */
    public static function envelopes(array $names): array
    {
        $out = [];
        foreach ($names as $name) {
            $reason = self::envelopeReason($name);
            if ($reason !== null) {
                $out[$name] = $reason;
            }
        }

        return $out;
    }

    /**
     * Every catalogued method that cannot be called, name => reason.
     *
     * @param  list<string>  $names
     * @return array<string, string>
     */
    public static function unsupported(array $names): array
    {
        $out = [];
        foreach ($names as $name) {
            $reason = self::reason($name);
            if ($reason !== null) {
                $out[$name] = $reason;
            }
        }

        return $out;
    }

    /**
     * constructor name => its line, from the catalogue's generating sources.
     * Only the `---functions---` section counts, exactly as the generator
     * requires: a TYPE (`communities.peerLinkRequests`) is not a method and
     * must not be reported as an uncallable one.
     *
     * @return array<string, array{file: string, role: string, line: string}>
     */
    private static function catalogueIndex(): array
    {
        if (self::$catalogueIndex !== null) {
            return self::$catalogueIndex;
        }

        $index = [];
        foreach (self::CATALOGUE_SOURCES as $file => $role) {
            foreach (self::functionLines($file) as $line) {
                $name = self::constructorNameOf($line);
                if ($name === '' || isset($index[$name])) {
                    continue;
                }
                $index[$name] = ['file' => $file, 'role' => $role, 'line' => $line];
            }
        }

        return self::$catalogueIndex = $index;
    }

    /**
     * constructor name => true, for every id-bearing non-generic line of the
     * files TLRegistry loads. Same admissibility rules as
     * `TLRegistry::mirrorFileLines()`: comments, section markers and id-less
     * cosmetic lines are skipped, and `{`-bearing generic wrappers are skipped
     * because the loader skips them.
     *
     * @return array<string, true>
     */
    private static function wireMirrorIndex(): array
    {
        if (self::$wireMirrorIndex !== null) {
            return self::$wireMirrorIndex;
        }

        $index = [];
        foreach (self::WIRE_MIRROR_FILES as $file) {
            foreach (self::mirrorLines($file) as $line) {
                $index[self::constructorNameOf($line)] = true;
            }
        }

        return self::$wireMirrorIndex = $index;
    }

    /**
     * Id-bearing, non-generic mirror lines. Mirrors the loader's own rule.
     *
     * @return list<string>
     */
    private static function mirrorLines(string $file): array
    {
        $out = [];
        foreach (self::rawLines($file) as $line) {
            $line = trim($line, " \t;");
            if ($line === '' || $line[0] === '/' || str_starts_with($line, '---')) {
                continue;
            }
            if (! str_contains($line, '#') || str_contains($line, '{')) {
                continue;
            }
            $out[] = $line;
        }

        return $out;
    }

    /**
     * Function-section lines of a catalogue source, `;` trimmed, the way the
     * generator reads them.
     *
     * @return list<string>
     */
    private static function functionLines(string $file): array
    {
        $inFunctions = false;
        $out = [];
        foreach (self::rawLines($file) as $line) {
            $line = trim($line);
            if ($line === '---functions---') {
                $inFunctions = true;

                continue;
            }
            if ($line === '---types---') {
                $inFunctions = false;

                continue;
            }
            if (! $inFunctions || $line === '' || $line[0] === '/' || str_starts_with($line, '---')) {
                continue;
            }
            $out[] = rtrim($line, ';');
        }

        return $out;
    }

    /**
     * @return list<string>
     */
    private static function rawLines(string $file): array
    {
        try {
            $path = SchemaArtifacts::source($file);
        } catch (\Throwable) {
            return [];
        }
        if (! is_file($path)) {
            return [];
        }

        return file($path, FILE_IGNORE_NEW_LINES | FILE_SKIP_EMPTY_LINES) ?: [];
    }

    /**
     * First space-delimited token, `#id` suffix stripped. String functions
     * only — the same extraction TLRegistry performs.
     */
    private static function constructorNameOf(string $line): string
    {
        $line = ltrim($line);
        $space = strpos($line, ' ');
        $token = $space === false ? $line : substr($line, 0, $space);
        $hash = strpos($token, '#');

        return $hash === false ? $token : substr($token, 0, $hash);
    }

    /**
     * The `// LAYER n` a source declares, read from the file itself. 0 when
     * the file declares none, which callers must read as "unknown", never as
     * "same as the other file".
     */
    public static function layerOf(string $file): int
    {
        if (isset(self::$layerCache[$file])) {
            return self::$layerCache[$file];
        }

        $layer = 0;
        foreach (self::rawLines($file) as $line) {
            $line = ltrim($line);
            if (str_starts_with($line, '// LAYER ')) {
                $layer = max($layer, (int) substr($line, 9));
            }
        }

        return self::$layerCache[$file] = $layer;
    }

    /** Test seam: drop the memoised indices. */
    public static function flush(): void
    {
        self::$catalogueIndex = null;
        self::$wireMirrorIndex = null;
        self::$layerCache = [];
    }
}
